--
Currently: BOTH assertions FAIL -- a wrong verdict on a correct program
(soundness-relevant, unlike the vacuous-drop KNOWNBUGs).  The source is
ten lines; only the libc++-23 headers are needed to reproduce, hence
the preprocessed carrier.
ROUND-90 DIAGNOSIS (bisected):
- An EMPTY vector is modelled correctly (size()==0, empty()).
- `reserve(4)` has NO EFFECT: capacity() is still 0 afterwards
  (asserting capacity()==0 SUCCEEDS), while size() stays correct.
- Root: `__vector_layout<T,A>::__relocate`, which reserve calls, is
  present in the symbol table with an EMPTY body and return type
  `auto (...) -> void` although it is declared `void` -- the OUT-OF-LINE
  definition (layout.h:290) was never matched to the in-class
  declaration (layout.h:196), so the odr-used member has no body
  ([temp.inst]/1 requires implicit instantiation when odr-used).
- The call is NOT flagged: no "no body for callee" property appears
  (CBMC does emit those elsewhere), so the state simply never changes
  and the wrong answer is SILENT.  That missing flag is a second,
  separable soundness gap.
- NEGATIVE KERNELS (round 90, both VERIFY clean): oo1 -- out-of-line
  definition of a class-template member whose parameter type is a
  MEMBER ALIAS; oo2 -- the same with `constexpr` plus libc++'s three
  attributes (visibility / exclude_from_explicit_instantiation /
  abi_tag) on the in-class declaration only.  So neither the member
  alias nor the attribute asymmetry is the trigger.
- REMAINING LEAD: the parameter type is
  `__split_buffer<T, A, __split_buffer_pointer_layout>`, and
  `__split_buffer` derives CRTP-style from its own template TEMPLATE
  parameter: `class __split_buffer : _Layout<__split_buffer<T,A,_Layout>,
  T, A>`.  That TT-parameter CRTP shape is the next thing to kernel.
